  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Find the radius and center of the circle whose equation is\n${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-3y=0.$",
  "worked_solution": "To find the center and radius of the circle, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-3y & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+({y}^{2}-3y) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+({y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}y+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}) & ={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the perfect square and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{(y-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} \\\\ (x-0)^{2}+{(y-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & ={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(0,\\frac{3}{2})$ and its radius is $\\frac{3}{2}.$",
